{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Among American X/Twitter users, is hostile political expression associated with political engagement and the factors that foster it, rather than being a phenomenon understood only through deprivation or personality?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We extend this revisionist\nperspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression\non X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Is the association between engagement and hostility stronger among users whose feelings toward political leaders are more polarized?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Expectation 2 concerns whether the associations between engagement and hostility are stronger among users with higher\nleader-directed affective polarization.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do users who express more political hostility also post more and have larger audiences or more connections on X/Twitter?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Online political hostility is associated with greater activity and potential reach on X/Twitter, measured by\nposting frequency and numbers of friends and followers.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Online political hostility is not confined to the kinds of users emphasized by deprivation, marginalization, or personality accounts. It is also associated with forms of engagement usually valued as part of democratic citizenship.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These\nfindings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based\nexplanations, as highlighted in prior work [14,15]. It is also associated with forms of engagement that are often considered\ncentral to democratic citizenship.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study links a six-wave U.S. YouGov panel to public X/Twitter activity with participants’ consent. Recruitment began in January 2019, an additional group joined in September 2020, and the last wave took place in January 2021. The linked sample contains 2,567 people, and X-only analyses can include up to 3,643.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All participants were recruited through YouGov’s standing web panel in the United States. Participation was voluntary,\nand participants provided informed digital consent at multiple stages.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We recruited the original set of panelists in January 2019.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "to provide their X ID and give consent to let us scrape their publicly available X data and link it to their survey answers.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "These\nparticipants, along with an additional set of panelists who were included to increase the study’s power in September 2020,\nwere then asked to participate in five additional survey waves, administered at various time points until the final survey in\nJanuary 2021.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period\nwas 2567. For the analyses where we only use X data the maximum number of respondents is 3643.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors selected the United States because they expected the associations to be especially visible in a setting with high affective polarization and political distrust.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The data collection focused on the United States specifically because the associations of interest may be especially\nvisible in highly polarized political environments. The United States provided an ideal context given its well-documented\nhigh levels of affective polarization and political distrust during the study period.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The behavioral corpus contains 9,250,180 posts collected from March 4, 2019, through January 2021. The unit of analysis is the individual panelist, not the post.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The scraping of X commenced on March 4, 2019 and stopped in January 2021. During this period, we managed to\nscrape 9,250,180 posts from our panelists. All measures based on X data are aggregated at the level of the individual\npanelist since our unit of analysis is individual panelists and not posts.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors define political hostility as degrading, malicious, or intimidating behavior directed at people or groups on the basis of political differences.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We define online political hostility as the expression of degrading, malicious, and\nintimidating online behavior towards individuals or collectives based on political\ndifferences.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The primary hostility measure uses Super-Unsupervised word embeddings trained on panelists’ posts. It combines the vectors for ‘political’ and ‘hate,’ scores posts by cosine similarity to that vector, and averages scores by user, or by user and wave or month in panel models.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our key measure of online political hostility is based on a word\nembeddings approach termed the Super-Unsupervised (SU) approach [60].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "First, we create word vectors based on posts from our panelists using the word2vec method [61,62].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Second, we combine the word vectors\n“political” and “hate” into a combined vector that captures the co-occurrence of political and hostile language in the corpus.\nThird, we use this combined measure to calculate the cosine similarity between individual posts and the “political hate”\nword vector:",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Fourth and finally, we create a\nmeasure of a respondent’s general level of political hostility by averaging the hostility scores of all their posts; for the panel\nmodels we compute this average per time unit investigated (wave or month, depending on the analysis).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For face validity, the earlier methods study inspected posts at both ends of the scale: high-scoring posts attacked political out-groups, whereas low-scoring posts were neutral or positive.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To assess the face validity of the overall measure [60], inspected the posts\nwith the highest and lowest political hostility scores.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The posts with the highest scores clearly contained hostile language\ndirected at political out-groups",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "In contrast, the posts with the lowest scores were either neutral or positive in tone and\ndid not contain any hostile language.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The primary score was compared with AFINN sentiment and Perspective API toxicity, both of which also serve as secondary outcomes. The primary score correlated with both and showed stronger correlations with political interest and knowledge.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "At the same time convergent validity was established through correlations with related measures: Google’s Perspective\nAPI toxicity scores (r = 0.59), and the AFINN sentiment lexicon (r = −0.62, negative because AFINN codes negativity as\nnegative values) [60].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "We know from [60] that the SU score correlates more strongly with, e.g., political interest\n(0.43) and political knowledge (0.44) than both AFINN (−0.28 and −0.29) and Perspective API toxicity scores (0.24\nand 0.22)",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "we also used two additional measures of online\nhostility or negativity.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Validation against 2,469 posts labeled by four expert raters yielded a reported held-out accuracy of 74% against a 50% baseline. A supervised classifier trained on expert labels was also used as a robustness check.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The SU approach was also validated against 2,469 hand-labeled posts rated by four expert raters",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Here, it reached 74% accuracy against a 50% baseline when dichotomized on a held-out test set.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "We use the supervised classifier as an additional\nrobustness check in S1 Appendix.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Criterion checks compared the most hostile posts to the most toxic posts and tested the score on an already labeled corpus. The most hostile posts featured political actors more often, and the score identified the rare hate-speech class more often than chance.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Among the 20,000 most politically hostile posts by SU, political actors and institutions are overrepresented: e.g., “Trump” appears 4,163 times vs 831 among the 20,000 most toxic (Perspective) posts",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "a simple percentile mapping of SU Hate yields balanced accuracy of.47 (chance = .33) and identifies the rare “hate\nspeech” class 3 × better than random",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "External validation compared scores obtained using public TwitterGloVe embeddings and tested transfer to a different task: comparing news-domain quality scores with expert fact-checker ratings.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "After aligning with public TwitterGloVe embeddings,\nSU “political hate” scores correlate r = .83 with scores computed in this external space",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "SU distances from (nytimes+washingtonpost) correlate .84 with expert fact-checker ratings at the domain\nlevel and .90 at the user-average level",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Ecological checks examined whether hostility language differed by party, whether the opposing party was typically the grammatical actor in high-scoring posts, and whether national hostility scores peaked on election day.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Democrats’ “political hate” space is relatively closer to “fearmongering/right-wing/Trumpian,”\nwhereas Republicans’ is closer to “left/Democrat,” matching well-known partisan schemata.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Dependency parsing of the\ntop 100k hostile posts shows the out-party as the grammatical actor in the large majority of cases",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "And in Denmark, Sweden, Germany, and Italy, national Political Hostility aggregates rise toward election day and peak\nexactly on that day, then decline, as expected if the measure tracks political conflict salience.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Education is measured in six attainment categories. Internal political efficacy is measured with two Center for Political Studies items and one European Social Survey item; the scale has an alpha of 0.70.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Education is measured using six different educational categories: No HS, High school graduate, Some college, 2-year,\n4-year, Post-grad.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Internal efficacy is measured using the standard Center for Political Studies (CPS) questions [46]",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "We also use a third item from the European Social\nSurvey that asks whether “I often think it is difficult to make up my mind about political issues.” Cronbach’s alpha is 0.70\nfor our measure of internal efficacy.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Political interest is measured with a five-point ANES self-rating; political knowledge is the number of correct answers to five factual ANES questions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Political interest is measured using a standard American National Election Studies (ANES) question asking respondents\nabout their level of political interest on a five-point scale ranging from “Extremely interested” to “Not interested at all.”",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Political knowledge is measured using five factual questions from the ANES, scored as the number of correct\nanswers.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Participation is self-reported on a seven-point frequency scale, with separate batteries for 11 offline and 10 online political activities.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To measure traditional offline participation, we created an 11-item battery\nbased on previous research [70,71], asking participants on scales ranging from “1 = Never” to “7 = Several times a day”\nhow often they participate in eleven offline political activities",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "To measure online participation, we asked participants on the same response scale how often\nthey engaged in ten online political activities",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Leader-directed affective polarization is the difference between average in-party and out-party ratings of three Democratic and three Republican politicians on a 0–100 feeling thermometer.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Respondents rated three Republican politicians (Ted Cruz, Donald Trump,\nand Mike Pence) and three Democratic politicians (Joe Biden, Elizabeth Warren, and Bernie Sanders) on a 0–100 feeling\nthermometer.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "We used respondents’ party identification to distinguish in-party from out-party leaders and calculated the\ndifference between average in-party and out-party leader ratings.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The main regressions adjust for Big Five personality traits, age, gender, income, and party identification. The traits come from a 44-item inventory, party identification from a seven-point ANES item, and demographics from YouGov’s panel records.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All models control for the Big\nFive personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "We rely on the Big Five Inventory (BFI) consisting of 44 items to measure the five major personality traits [76].",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Party identification is measured using a standard question from the American National\nElection Study on a seven-point scale ranging from “Strong Democrat” to “Strong Republican.” Standard demographics of\nage, gender and income were obtained from YouGov’s background information about its panelists.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The pooled cross-sectional regressions use OLS with clustered standard errors to account for repeated observations of users. Questions and participation vary by wave, and the authors use multiple imputation and full-information maximum likelihood for missing data.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The six surveys did not include the same questions,\nand, consequently, not all measures described below are available in each wave. Further, as participants were free to opt\nin and out of the different surveys, the final sample size for each wave differs.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "For the cross-sectional analyses, we use ordinary least squares (OLS) regression with cluster robust standard errors to\naccount for the fact that a single X user can participate across multiple waves. This dataset is organized as a Time-Series\nCross-Section (TSCS) dataset using pooled OLS.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "we therefore\nuse multiple imputation and full information maximum likelihood to estimate missing values in these analyses.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Robustness analyses separate the ‘political’ and ‘hate’ components, test whether general toxicity and sentiment behave differently as posts become more politically relevant, and control for each user’s share of political posts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the composite dependent variable of online political hostility is\nsubdivided into the two components of “political” and “hate” (i.e., general hostility).",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "we also created a model where we interact the Perspective API toxicity scores and AFINN\nscores with a political relevance score (based on the cosine similarity between individual posts and a “politics” word vector\ncreated using the same word embedding approach as described above).",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, we have also created a model where we control for the share of political posts among all posts posted by\neach user.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The moderation analysis compares users above and below the polarization scale midpoint and tests formal interaction terms. These models adjust for age and gender, but omit the full set of controls to reduce multicollinearity.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Fig 4 displays the associations separately for users above and below the midpoint\nof the leader-directed affective-polarization scale.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "All models control for age and gender but not the full set of controls to reduce problems of multicollinearity. The figure illustrates the interaction term from\neach model from the interaction between the engagement indicators and leader-directed affective polarization.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Random-intercept cross-lagged panel models use behavioral measures of online engagement and hostility to distinguish changes within users over time from stable differences between users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the panel analyses, we use Random-Intercept Cross-Lagged Panel Models (RI-CLPM). These models separate\nstable between-person differences from within-person change",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "we use RI-CLPMs with behavioral measures of online political engagement and online\nhostility.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The exploratory reach analysis uses negative-binomial models for post counts, accounts followed, and followers. The appendix also visualizes the sample’s follower/friend network descriptively.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "these are negative-binomial count models, and their estimates are\nreported on the original count scale.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "those who are politically hostile (a) post more, (b) have more friends, and (c) have more followers. A “friend” in X’s usage\nis someone I follow, and a “follower” is someone who follows me.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "we include exploratory descriptive\nnetwork visualizations of the study participants’ follower/friend graph in S1 Appendix",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors adjust for multiple testing separately within four model families, applying Benjamini–Hochberg false-discovery-rate corrections and reporting Bonferroni-adjusted 95% confidence intervals.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We define four separate families of tests: the primary association models,\nthe robustness outcome models, the affective-polarization interaction models, and the exploratory activity/reach models.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Within each family we apply a Benjamini–Hochberg false-discovery-rate (FDR) correction and, for the more conservative\nreading requested by the reviewers, also report Bonferroni-adjusted 95% confidence intervals.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "The strongest positive associations with political hostility are for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation. The main associations persist after adjustment for broad personality traits, demographics, and party identification.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the\nstrongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "The associations were obtained after controlling for\nbroad personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The main pattern is not unique to the primary hostility score and is not explained simply by engaged users posting more about politics. The component and supervised measures yield broadly similar results, and controlling for the political share of posts does not weaken the main pattern.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The robustness analyses are broadly consistent with the main pattern, with significant and sizeable associations across\nall three measures.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "the main results are robust to this\nadditional control; if anything the results are slightly stronger when controlling for the share of political posts. This suggests that the results are not simply driven by more politically engaged users posting more about politics.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Stronger engagement–hostility associations among users with more polarized feelings toward leaders are clearest for online and offline participation. Eight of 18 interaction terms survive false-discovery-rate correction, concentrated among the participation measures.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "However, the moderation evidence is not uniform across all indicators. Formal interaction models (Fig 3) show\nthe clearest support for online and offline participation",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "of the eighteen interaction terms, eight\nremain significant after a Benjamini–Hochberg correction, and these are concentrated among the participatory engagement indicators.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Within users, increases in behavioral online engagement precede increases in hostility on all three measures. There is some evidence of the reverse sequence, but it is weaker and less consistent.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "within-person increases in online political engagement\nare followed by higher levels of online political hostility across the three hostility measures. We also observe some evidence of the reverse temporal association, in which hostility is followed by higher political engagement, although this\npattern is weaker and less consistent.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "In exploratory count models, more hostile users post more and have more followers and, to a lesser extent, follow more accounts. Moving from mean hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above it is associated with an increase from roughly 420 to 900 posts and from 220 to 370 followers.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The count-model results indicate that higher political hostility is associated with more posts and with larger numbers\nof followers and, to a lesser extent, friends.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above\nthe mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the\nnumber of followers from around 220 to around 370.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The study adds an engagement-based account without deciding between socioeconomic grievance accounts and accounts based on personality or emotion regulation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The present study does not seek to adjudicate between these literatures.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study excludes nonpolitical toxicity and does not claim to measure the narrower construct of hate speech or to offer the only way to measure hostility.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Accordingly, toxic online exchanges in the aftermath of, say, a\nsports game fall outside the purview of what we examine here.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we do not wish to confuse this more general measure of online political hostility with the narrower concept of “hate speech” which we are not purporting to measure.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "We thus do not\nmean to imply that the ways of measuring online hostility presented here are the only ways to measure it.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Findings from U.S. X/Twitter users may not carry over to platforms with different users, features, moderation, or norms. The appendix provides tentative evidence from Danish X/Twitter users, not a test of other platforms.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the associations observed here may not generalize directly to platforms with different affordances, audiences, moderation systems, or norms of interaction.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Some tentative evidence of broader applicability is provided in S1 Appendix, where online political hostility in a sample of\nDanish X/Twitter users is also positively associated with users’ political interest, internal efficacy, and news consumption,\nand where the relationship extends – to a lesser extent – to users with negative views of the political system [14,50].",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The observational analyses do not establish that engagement causes hostility. The within-user models address stable confounding but cannot rule out time-varying confounding, measurement error, reciprocal effects, or common causes such as political sophistication or partisan involvement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The RI-CLPM analyses reduce concerns about stable time-invariant confounding\nby focusing on within-person change, but they do not rule out time-varying confounding, reciprocal relationships, or measurement error. Political sophistication or partisan involvement may be common causes of both participation and hostility.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "The findings should overall be interpreted as associational rather than causal.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The analyses do not identify the causal pathways among the engagement indicators or show that education makes users more hostile independently of their generally higher engagement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the present findings are mute on the exact causal relationships between the different indicators of political engagement and their antecedents.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "the analyses should not be taken to imply\nthat, for example, more educated users are more hostile absent their generally higher levels of political engagement.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The embedding score is an imperfect measure: it can pick up politically salient conflict language or hate-related vocabulary as well as hostility itself.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "no automated text-based measure perfectly captures the underlying construct. The measure may partly capture\nproximity to politically salient conflict language or hate-related political vocabulary rather than hostility alone.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The moderation results concern feelings toward political leaders, not parties as institutions or ordinary supporters. Whether the same pattern holds for those other targets is untested.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "our measure of affective polarization captures feelings toward party elites rather than\ntoward parties as institutions or ordinary party supporters, as is standard [22].",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Future research should examine whether the same\nconditional patterns appear using party-directed or supporter-directed measures.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The interaction results are descriptive: they do not show that polarization causally changes the effect of engagement on hostility, and the conditional pattern is exploratory for indicators without robust support.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We therefore interpret the moderation analyses as descriptive\nevidence about conditional associations rather than as evidence that affective polarization causally moderates the effect\nof engagement on hostility.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "The conditional pattern should therefore be treated as exploratory for the remaining indicators.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Big Five controls do not directly test explanations based on Dark Triad traits, sadism, or other specific antisocial traits, which the surveys did not measure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These controls are not intended as a direct test of Dark Triad,\nsadism, or other antagonistic-trait accounts of online hostility. The survey did not include measures of those more specific\ntraits, and we therefore cannot adjudicate those accounts directly.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The activity and audience measures indicate potential reach, not formal network influence, centrality, or community structure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This implications notwithstanding, we do\nnot draw conclusions about formal network influence, centrality, or community structure.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors make data and code for the main analyses available on OSF, but cannot publicly release raw posts or identifier-bearing network files because participants could be re-identified.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Data and scripts to reproduce the main analyses can be found on OSF.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Raw\npost-level X/Twitter data and identifier-bearing\nauxiliary network files cannot be made publicly\navailable because they contain information that\ncould create re-identification risks for survey\nparticipants.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors build on classical political-engagement theory but distinguish resources that facilitate engagement from motivation, knowledge, and participation itself. They analyze these separately rather than assuming a single pathway.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Building on classical theories of political engagement [17,19] and recent work on the darker sides of engagement, we\nfind that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "First, education and internal efficacy are distal antecedents of political engagement. Second, political interest and political\nknowledge are motivational and cognitive indicators of engagement. Third, online and offline political participation are\nbehavioral indicators of engagement.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "we will\nin the present study treat them as separate indicators as they may not operate through the same pathways.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The paper extends the revisionist tradition on the costs of political engagement, including work on motivated reasoning and polarization, from biased information processing and misinformation sharing to hostile political expression online.",
